{"duration_api_ms":73698,"stop_reason":"tool_use","session_id":"f5f6d22d-f2e6-43b7-a527-813a736f28a5","total_cost_usd":0.4566816,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":79542,"cache_read_input_tokens":83463,"output_tokens":6936,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":2243},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":79542,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2371,"cache_read_input_tokens":79141,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4723,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4723},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":52363,"outputTokens":18,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.052453,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"thinkingTokens":0,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-4-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"},"claude-sonnet-5":{"inputTokens":4,"outputTokens":6936,"cacheReadInputTokens":83463,"cacheCreationInputTokens":79542,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.4042286,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"thinkingTokens":2243,"canonicalModel":"claude-sonnet-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":3,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"requirements\":[{\"id\":\"req-1\",\"requirement\":\"Configure node1, node2, node3 as a three-member MariaDB Galera cluster with writes through any member visible from the other members\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"node1 bootstrapped a new cluster (cluster_size=1, Synced, Primary) via galera_new_cluster after switching to MariaDB 10.4 + galera-4 (the stock 10.0 build lacked WSREP support, confirmed by 'unknown variable wsrep_on'). node2 and node3 joined via SST and all three report wsrep_cluster_size=3 / Synced. A write on node1 (id=3) appeared on node2 and node3; a write on node2 (id=4) and a write on node3 (id=5) both appeared identically on all three nodes when re-queried, directly demonstrating multi-master write propagation in all directions.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-49e81488576846f0\",\"cmd-89be38dea5af4690\",\"cmd-cab6093158624c69\",\"cmd-106040ee094f4101\",\"cmd-5d1ef2b24d514e3d\",\"cmd-d7d7a45a7fcc43d5\",\"cmd-4ffe158488524cbd\",\"cmd-8b6ab370c22c4fa9\",\"cmd-df22099db3514c94\",\"cmd-6c5e1abb85b1496f\"]},{\"id\":\"req-2\",\"requirement\":\"Keep the database available (writable) when one member is unavailable\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"With cluster healthy at size 3 (baseline shown in cmd-106040ee094f4101 etc.), node2's mariadb was stopped (cmd-bd0fa5a62c474cb4). node1 and node3 both then reported wsrep_cluster_size=2 and wsrep_cluster_status=Primary, and both successfully accepted new writes (node1 inserted 'n1-down2', node3 inserted 'n3-down2') while node2 remained down, directly showing the cluster stayed available and writable during the bounded disruption.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-bd0fa5a62c474cb4\",\"cmd-307514bef7ba418a\",\"cmd-8c5c3b3789fa4be2\",\"cmd-5bb08c0bf0bd4ea1\",\"cmd-7790986252dd4681\"]},{\"id\":\"req-3\",\"requirement\":\"Automatically resynchronize a member when it returns after being unavailable\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"After node2 was restarted (cmd-11fe3dddca844124), it rejoined automatically without manual SST invocation, reporting wsrep_cluster_size=3 and wsrep_local_state_comment=Synced, and a SELECT on node2 showed all rows written during its outage ('n1-down2' id=6, 'n3-down2' id=9) present, directly demonstrating automatic resync and data recovery.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-11fe3dddca844124\"]}],\"confidence\":0.92,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.8,\"summary\":\"The final state is clean: the after_executor lifecycle snapshot shows no failed systemd units, no residual listening-socket anomalies, and consistent dpkg package health on all three nodes; only expected reboot-related /tmp systemd-private directories differ. However, the executor's path to the working solution included several state-changing detours that were not strictly necessary for the final outcome: (1) an initial full install of stock mariadb-server-10.0 + galera-3 on all three nodes (cmd-a414ab36d5a849e7, cmd-3221128b6d0e486c, cmd-d4ea07f76a7b4ffa) that was later found to lack WSREP support and had to be removed/upgraded away when installing galera-4/mariadb 10.4 (cmd-35770d21946d43aa, cmd-ddb0f9f273964f1c, cmd-4615dd7d1aed429e) — a bounded, self-corrected mutation with no lasting residue; (2) repeated failed bootstrap attempts on node1 using mysqld_safe backgrounding and a transient systemd-run unit 'mysql-bootstrap' that exited with status 7 (cmd-afc94b84d5704ab4, cmd-bde9c548b5574c48, cmd-ebf5d954160c4bd1), which were diagnostic/troubleshooting mutations (starting/stopping mysqld processes, a failed transient unit later reset via reset-failed) that did not directly contribute to the eventual successful galera_new_cluster bootstrap. These were bounded, low-risk, and fully reverted/superseded, so they are treated as minor rather than severe hygiene issues.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-a414ab36d5a849e7\",\"cmd-3221128b6d0e486c\",\"cmd-d4ea07f76a7b4ffa\",\"cmd-35770d21946d43aa\",\"cmd-ddb0f9f273964f1c\",\"cmd-4615dd7d1aed429e\",\"cmd-afc94b84d5704ab4\",\"cmd-bde9c548b5574c48\",\"cmd-ebf5d954160c4bd1\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node2:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node3:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node1:package-health\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"All three material task outcomes — a working three-member Galera cluster with bidirectional write replication, availability/writability with one member down, and automatic resynchronization on return — are directly demonstrated by chronologically ordered, first-party command evidence (status queries and SELECTs showing actual row propagation, not just configuration). The mandatory provider-wide restart protocol was also completed for all three nodes with boot-id changes cross-checked against the fixed after-prepare/after-executor lifecycle snapshots, and each node returned to cluster_size=3/Synced with full data intact after its individual reboot. The executor's route to the solution involved discovering that Ubuntu's stock mariadb-server-10.0 lacked WSREP support and switching to the official MariaDB 10.4 xenial repo with galera-4, which required some install/uninstall churn and a few failed bootstrap attempts; these were bounded, diagnostic in nature, and left no residue in the final state.\",\"limitations\":[\"Evidence for req-2/req-3 covers only a single member-down/resync cycle (node2); node1 and node3 were not individually taken down and resynced to demonstrate the same behavior for every node, though this is a reasonable single representative test.\",\"The restart-protocol post-restart checks each query only wsrep_cluster_size/state/status and row COUNT, not a full data diff across all nodes after each individual restart (only the final cross-node check after all three restarts includes a full row listing).\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"req-1","requirement":"Configure node1, node2, node3 as a three-member MariaDB Galera cluster with writes through any member visible from the other members","status":"satisfied","summary":"node1 bootstrapped a new cluster (cluster_size=1, Synced, Primary) via galera_new_cluster after switching to MariaDB 10.4 + galera-4 (the stock 10.0 build lacked WSREP support, confirmed by 'unknown variable wsrep_on'). node2 and node3 joined via SST and all three report wsrep_cluster_size=3 / Synced. A write on node1 (id=3) appeared on node2 and node3; a write on node2 (id=4) and a write on node3 (id=5) both appeared identically on all three nodes when re-queried, directly demonstrating multi-master write propagation in all directions.","evidence_ids":["cmd-49e81488576846f0","cmd-89be38dea5af4690","cmd-cab6093158624c69","cmd-106040ee094f4101","cmd-5d1ef2b24d514e3d","cmd-d7d7a45a7fcc43d5","cmd-4ffe158488524cbd","cmd-8b6ab370c22c4fa9","cmd-df22099db3514c94","cmd-6c5e1abb85b1496f"]},{"id":"req-2","requirement":"Keep the database available (writable) when one member is unavailable","status":"satisfied","summary":"With cluster healthy at size 3 (baseline shown in cmd-106040ee094f4101 etc.), node2's mariadb was stopped (cmd-bd0fa5a62c474cb4). node1 and node3 both then reported wsrep_cluster_size=2 and wsrep_cluster_status=Primary, and both successfully accepted new writes (node1 inserted 'n1-down2', node3 inserted 'n3-down2') while node2 remained down, directly showing the cluster stayed available and writable during the bounded disruption.","evidence_ids":["cmd-bd0fa5a62c474cb4","cmd-307514bef7ba418a","cmd-8c5c3b3789fa4be2","cmd-5bb08c0bf0bd4ea1","cmd-7790986252dd4681"]},{"id":"req-3","requirement":"Automatically resynchronize a member when it returns after being unavailable","status":"satisfied","summary":"After node2 was restarted (cmd-11fe3dddca844124), it rejoined automatically without manual SST invocation, reporting wsrep_cluster_size=3 and wsrep_local_state_comment=Synced, and a SELECT on node2 showed all rows written during its outage ('n1-down2' id=6, 'n3-down2' id=9) present, directly demonstrating automatic resync and data recovery.","evidence_ids":["cmd-11fe3dddca844124"]}],"confidence":0.92,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.8,"summary":"The final state is clean: t