tly demonstrated by chronologically ordered, first-party command evidence (status queries and SELECTs showing actual row propagation, not just configuration). The mandatory provider-wide restart protocol was also completed for all three nodes with boot-id changes cross-checked against the fixed after-prepare/after-executor lifecycle snapshots, and each node returned to cluster_size=3/Synced with full data intact after its individual reboot. The executor's route to the solution involved discovering that Ubuntu's stock mariadb-server-10.0 lacked WSREP support and switching to the official MariaDB 10.4 xenial repo with galera-4, which required some install/uninstall churn and a few failed bootstrap attempts; these were bounded, diagnostic in nature, and left no residue in the final state.","limitations":["Evidence for req-2/req-3 covers only a single member-down/resync cycle (node2); node1 and node3 were not individually taken down and resynced to demonstrate the same behavior for every node, though this is a reasonable single representative test.","The restart-protocol post-restart checks each query only wsrep_cluster_size/state/status and row COUNT, not a full data diff across all nodes after each individual restart (only the final cross-node check after all three restarts includes a full row listing)."]},"ttft_ms":30547,"type":"result","duration_ms":72200,"uuid":"c2039c7c-329f-4607-9ece-8bdfa3430596","ttft_stream_ms":1364,"time_to_request_ms":42,"first_content_frame_ms":1905,"queued_turn_count":0}
